00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.670
LAURA JONES: Hello, and
welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.000
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.440
We're hosting webinars daily.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.920
My name is Laura Jones, and
I'm from the Chrome Enterprise

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.000
team.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.800
And today we'll be
talking about how

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.820
to turn your frontline
workers into cloud workers.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.620
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.100
and we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.080
Let's get started.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.420
So first, you might
be wondering, well,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.160
what's a cloud worker?

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.280
So let's start out there.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.490
Now, it's fair to say that
the cloud has fundamentally

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.710
changed the way that
many of us interact

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.500
with technology in
our personal lives.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.470
We're used to a world where,
at a click of a button,

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:16.390
I can stream music or movies
to any device that I want.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.420
I can order up a car to
meet me exactly where I am

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.610
and take me exactly
where I'm going.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.670
It's technology
that works instantly

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.040
that's easy to use
that's seamless

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.830
across all of my devices.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.680
And all of this interaction
with cloud technology

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.910
in our personal lives has raised
the expectations for the way

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:40.330
that technology
should work at work.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.450
And there's many
employees today that

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:47.320
are using cloud technology
to do the vast majority

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:51.250
of their work, and they're
preferring that as well.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:54.340
And we call these
workers cloud workers.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.050
And they make up today about
25% of the employee population,

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:02.680
but we think that that's
just going to grow over time.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.480
These cloud workers value speed,
convenience, collaboration,

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.220
and fluidity of work.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:14.480
They want to be able to work
wherever, whenever they like.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.480
Now, when you think
about a cloud worker,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.820
you might think, OK, I
know who these people are.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.260
They work in technology.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.160
They're probably in marketing.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.220
But the fact is
that cloud workers

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.250
span beyond traditional
role segments.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.880
It's not about where you
work, but about how you work.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.430
And we think that one
segment of the workforce that

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.700
could benefit the most
from cloud technology,

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.740
and perhaps could be the easiest
to move to cloud technology,

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.920
are frontline workers.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.275
So let's talk about what we mean
when we say frontline workers.

00:03:49.275 --> 00:03:50.900
These are the workers
that are building

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.110
the products that people,
customers interact with.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.690
They're the people
in front of customers

00:03:56.690 --> 00:04:00.140
who are impacting the
brand every single day,

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.090
and they're often deskless.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:06.570
They're cashiers, nurses,
call center agents,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.040
people on the
manufacturing line.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.010
80% of the global
workforce is deskless,

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:17.269
and many of these deskless
workers are frontline workers.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:20.209
Frontline workers build
up a wealth of knowledge

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:22.580
from the routine and
essential services

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.530
that they provide every day.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.790
And this is knowledge that is
critical for a headquarters,

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:32.150
for a brand to be
able to tap into.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.520
They're the backbone
of the company,

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.070
and they're the
face of the company.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.520
Whether building the product or
directly in front of customers,

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.270
frontline workers
have a direct impact

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:46.340
on the customer
experience, which

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:49.070
impacts the bottom line too.

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:51.230
Think about the last time
you had an interaction

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:52.460
with a brand.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.480
You were probably interacting
with a frontline worker.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.660
You were at a restaurant
and had great service.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.630
You were speaking to
a call center agent

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:05.190
and had maybe bad service.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:08.870
How did that impact the way that
you thought about that brand?

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.760
The reality is that
it probably had

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.240
a lot more impact than
the advertisements

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.740
that we see every day.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:18.920
And this brand
interaction is extremely

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:20.720
important to the business.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.100
According to HBR, customers who
have positive experiences spend

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:31.076
140% more with the brand
than those who don't.

00:05:31.076 --> 00:05:34.340
But even though they have
this outsized impact,

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:37.040
frontline workers are
often left behind,

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.630
and their full potential remains
largely untapped because of it.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:45.740
According to another HBR
survey, only 12% of leaders

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.690
believe their
organizations invest

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:50.740
sufficiently enough in
the frontline managers who

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:52.670
are the people
training and making

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:55.880
sure that the frontline
workers, that the servers,

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.380
that the call center agents
are able to do their jobs well.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:02.720
And frontline workers
who have to deal

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.940
with a lot of
communication don't

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.520
feel like they have the
tools to be able to manage

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.590
that communication.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:13.400
And even more, 75%
of frontline workers

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.350
feel their employers
aren't telling them

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:19.150
enough about the changes
in policies and goals.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:20.230
So think about that.

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:23.560
If these are the people that are
meant to represent your brand,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.960
and they don't know enough about
what's going on at the company

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:28.720
and what the
company's mission is,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:30.910
they can't do their
jobs as effectively

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:34.590
as they should be able to be.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:36.690
We've also seen that
they're being left behind

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.740
by technology.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:42.720
We did a survey at Google where
we asked information workers

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.020
and frontline workers
whether the technology

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.870
in their workplace has changed
over the past five years.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:52.580
And what we found is
that information workers,

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:55.020
70% of them said yes.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:58.420
And I think that's
pretty standard.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.290
But when we asked frontline
workers, only 50% of them

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.520
said yes.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.640
Think about how much technology
has changed over the past five

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.570
years.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.540
And only one in two
frontline workers

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.492
are benefiting from
the technology advances

00:07:12.492 --> 00:07:13.950
that information
workers have seen.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:22.670
So the disengagement that
comes because frontline workers

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.950
aren't getting
enough investment,

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:27.420
aren't getting the
right technology--

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.300
it has a high
price for companies

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.670
and for the economy as a whole.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:34.400
According to a McKinsey
study, frontline workers

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.680
are more pessimistic
than their managers

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:40.040
about leadership and motivation.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:42.650
And we've also seen,
just as a standard,

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:47.900
that store employees
turnover was 65%, much higher

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.310
than the industry average.

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:53.520
That turnover, coupled with
potentially lost revenue

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.000
because a frontline worker isn't
as informed about something

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.620
that they need to be--

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:01.410
that all can create
disengaged employees

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:07.080
that cost the US economy
between $483 to $605 billion

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.900
in lost productivity every year.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:13.050
So it's a real problem.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.310
So we've talked about
the outsized impact

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.890
that frontline workers have,
and we've talked about the fact

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:21.470
that they might not be
getting enough investment.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:24.640
So it's clear to us at Google
that digitally empowered

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:27.340
frontline workers
aren't a luxury,

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.370
but they're a necessity in
today's fast-paced moving

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.080
digital economy.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.350
And we think that cloud
can unlock the potential

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:37.980
of this workforce.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:41.190
On one hand, it creates a
better employee experience

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:43.320
by giving employees more
information that they

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.360
need to do their
job, by ensuring

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.640
that they're engaged
and connected

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.560
with the organization.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.630
And those happy
employees are then

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:55.920
able to provide better customer
service and experience.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:57.600
Along with that,
cloud technology

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.360
can be used for things
like personalization

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:03.750
or better processes that make
it smoother for the customer

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.040
to interact with your brand--

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:09.000
things like self-service,
for instance.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.750
And cloud has stronger
security and increased value.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:20.510
Chrome Enterprise, the
OS devices and browser,

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.850
is the right choice
for your organization

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:25.130
when thinking about
how to put the power

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.010
of the cloud in the hands
of your frontline workers.

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:32.840
It has benefits for both
workers and for your business.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:37.130
For frontline workers, it can
increase access to information.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:41.390
You can put applications
through the Google Play Store

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:43.610
into the hands of your
frontline workers.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:47.160
They can access applications
via the web browser.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:51.650
And if you use VDI for both
desktop and app virtualization,

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:54.800
that works with Chrome
devices as well.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.920
Chrome devices are
fast and familiar.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:09:59.840
One of the things that
we hear a lot when

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:04.250
talking to our customers is
that frontline workers might not

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.533
be as digitally savvy as
an information worker,

00:10:07.533 --> 00:10:09.200
and so they need
something that's really

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.510
easy to onboard for employees.

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.420
Also, they're onboarding a
lot of employees at a time,

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.960
so they need something,
again, that's easy to use.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:21.530
Chrome OS is built on
a browser foundation,

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:23.090
and people know how
to use a browser.

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:26.460
So it's just quick and
instant for them to use.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.480
And there's a versatility
of devices for any use case

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.271
that you need them for.

00:10:31.271 --> 00:10:35.190
For your business, Chrome
Enterprise is secure by design.

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.650
And I'll talk more
about that in a bit.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.410
It's also easy to
share and manage.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.070
And the big benefit here
is frontline workers

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:45.200
are in a bunch of
different locations,

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.630
spread out, not
always at headquarters

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:49.790
and able to interact with IT.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:53.540
And so when IT can remotely
manage, they can scale.

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.670
And we've heard from
a lot of our customers

00:10:55.670 --> 00:11:00.920
that they can go from 200
to 600 employees over time

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:03.830
while maintaining the
same set of IT resources

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:06.710
because it's that
easy to manage.

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:10.770
And Chrome Enterprise
is a smart investment.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:15.130
So let's talk about the benefits
to the frontline workers first.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:18.720
First of all, it helps them
get their valuable time back.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.220
Now, we've all been there where
we're waiting for our device

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:23.620
to boot, or we're waiting
for an update to happen.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:26.300
And that's lost
productivity time.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.000
Chromebooks boot in
six seconds or less.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:32.570
I just open up my laptop, and
I can get to work right away.

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:35.200
This is one of, for me
personally, the best

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.850
things about Chromebooks.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:38.810
And updates happen
in the background,

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:42.700
so I don't have to sit there
waiting for 30 minutes,

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:45.640
waiting for that
progress bar to go around

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:48.740
for my update to start for
me to get back to work.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:49.882
It just happens seamlessly.

00:11:49.882 --> 00:11:51.340
I don't even know
what's happening,

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.190
but I know my device is secure.

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:57.610
And cloud-based profiles
enable a consistent experience

00:11:57.610 --> 00:11:59.180
across any device.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.130
So I can be a mobile worker
walking the hospital floor,

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:05.320
and I can log in to
any device that's

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:08.470
near me with my cloud profile,
and I get the experience

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:09.640
that I need.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.970
Or if I've lost my device
or my device is broken,

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.160
I just pick up another
Chromebook, sign in,

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:17.680
and I'm ready to get working.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:22.420
And with on-device AI and ML,
I can also find my applications

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:23.672
much faster.

00:12:23.672 --> 00:12:25.630
That way, I have the
information in front of me

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:27.190
that I need to do my job.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:34.160
And Chrome devices have a
wide variety of form factors

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:37.130
and come from all
the major OEMs.

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:40.550
So there's tablets
for a store associate

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:45.010
who's roaming the floors
and helping out customers.

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:49.040
There is clam shells
and convertible devices

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:53.240
for employees who need a
device in a stationary location

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:54.560
or mobile workers.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.840
And there's, of course,
desktops for those employees

00:12:56.840 --> 00:12:59.990
who are at a desk all day
like a call center employee.

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:03.530
And all Chrome devices come
with a standard 10-plus hours

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:05.630
of battery life,
which means I can

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:09.890
be working all day long without
having to charge my device.

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:13.140
And we have form factors that
are more rugged and tough

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:15.620
for those environments that
need a solution like that.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.720
So let's talk about the
security of Chrome Enterprise.

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:24.180
This is an area that
our engineering team

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:27.300
spent a lot of time focused on.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.110
From the firmware all the
way through the applications,

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.350
Chromebooks, Chrome
OS, and Chrome browser

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:38.730
are secure by design with
multiple layers of security.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:43.380
This way, employees
fall to harmful attacks

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:45.160
like malware attacks.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:48.030
And if one does prevail,
it prevents that

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:51.570
from spreading across the OS
with things like sandboxing

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:54.090
and side isolation.

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:56.640
And with verified
boot, I'm ensured

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:02.160
that a tampered OS never boots
up on my operating system.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.800
And if you didn't know, there
are actually two operating

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.090
systems on a Chrome device.

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:11.090
So when I boot up my machine,
if one has been tampered with,

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:14.620
it will seamlessly boot up the
other OS in the background.

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:18.210
And this is great because we
hear a lot from IT departments

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:21.060
that they want to make
sure frontline workers have

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:22.800
a really secure device.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.380
Because first of all,
their hourly employees,

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:26.820
there is turnover.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:29.370
But also, frontline workers
are working with a lot

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.440
of customer and patient data.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:35.190
And it's really important
that that data remain secure

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:38.310
and that these employees
don't fall for harmful attacks

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.720
like phishing or malware.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:43.230
And then, of course, there's
regular and controlled updates

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:44.700
that happen in the background.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:48.473
Every six weeks, you can push
an update to your employees.

00:14:48.473 --> 00:14:49.890
You don't have to
worry about them

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:51.450
accepting the update or not.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:55.230
It just happens seamlessly
in the background.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:57.310
And with the Chrome
Enterprise license,

00:14:57.310 --> 00:15:00.650
you can manage your
entire fleet of devices.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:04.160
There's 200-plus user
and device policies

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:07.990
so you can make sure that your
employees only have access

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:09.670
to the applications
that they need,

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.770
that it's preloaded
with the information

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:13.450
that you wanted to have.

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:17.800
You could even do things like
lock down the on-device drive

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.410
so that employees can't
save any information

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:24.670
to the actual device, and
it's secure in the cloud.

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:26.350
And the Chrome
Enterprise license

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:29.560
allows you to work with
your current infrastructure,

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:32.560
so integrating with things
like your Enterprise Mobility

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:35.080
Management solution
or the identity

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:36.980
that you choose to have.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:39.250
And of course, it comes
with 24/7 support.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:44.660
One of the areas
in IT management

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:48.540
that Google and our customers
have seen lots of benefits for

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:52.170
are in significant deployment
and support savings.

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:55.010
So Google has a mixed ecosystem.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:59.840
We use Windows devices, MacOS,
and of course, Chrome OS.

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:02.210
And what our internal
teams have seen

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:05.660
is that a Chrome OS
device takes 33 minutes

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:09.950
to deploy compared to two hours
and 21 minutes with a Windows

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:10.950
device.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.820
Think about all the devices
that you have to deploy over

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:15.080
a period of time.

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:18.560
That's significant savings
for the IT department.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:21.180
And then when we look
at support savings,

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:24.680
there's only 19
tickets for Chrome OS

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:30.560
for every 1,000 active devices
compared to 95 for Windows.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.560
So less things are going
wrong on the Chrome OS

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:36.290
devices that the IT
department has to handle.

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:41.960
The other really special
thing about Chrome OS

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:45.180
is the unparalleled
multi-user experience.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:46.620
So this means a few things.

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:50.300
One-- I could securely
share devices between users

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.000
through encrypted
cloud profiles.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.220
If I'm using ephemeral
mode, as soon

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:58.910
as an employee signs out of the
device, the device is wiped,

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:01.652
and it's then ready for the
next employee to sign into.

00:17:01.652 --> 00:17:04.069
And this is really important
when you think about the fact

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:06.829
that frontline workers
could be sharing one device,

00:17:06.829 --> 00:17:09.619
up to 10 employees
using that device.

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:11.720
And you don't have to
worry about anything

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:15.390
being left on the device when
the next person signs in.

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:17.450
It also means that
I can flexibly

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:19.940
configure my devices
for whatever use case

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:22.069
that I need in an instant.

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:24.290
So I could take this
Chromebook today,

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.240
and use it for
customer self-service,

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:29.690
and manage Guest Mode, and I
could deploy it in a few hours

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:32.390
to an information worker
who needs to use it

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:35.120
for their regular business.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.100
And I can sign in
to any Chromebook

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:38.880
to pick up where I've left off.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.243
So I talked about this before,
but if my device were to break,

00:17:42.243 --> 00:17:43.910
I could just pick up
another Chromebook,

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:48.900
sign in, use it instantly,
and be productive.

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.620
Now, all of this-- the
productivity from the end user,

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:56.340
the decreased IT resources
needed, as well as

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:59.370
the hardware and
software cost avoidance--

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:04.030
that adds up to big
benefits for the business.

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:08.550
We conducted a total economic
impact study with Forester.

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:11.160
And according to the
composite organization

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:14.310
using shared Chromebook
devices, they

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:17.580
realized, over three
years, $1.5 million

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:21.900
in hardware and software
cost avoidance, $3.5 million

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:26.820
in employee productivity
gains, and a 75% improvement

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:28.200
in IT productivity.

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:32.690
Now, depending on
your use case, you

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:35.810
might also want to add
on a productivity suite.

00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:39.560
And Chromebook works with
anything that you need it to.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:41.990
But we recommend,
of course, G Suite

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:45.470
to help with collaboration
and communication.

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:48.470
Using Drive and Docs,
Sheets, and Slides,

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:51.770
you can share information
to your employees,

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:54.320
and they can easily
consume it in the cloud.

00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:56.180
And they can also
share information back.

00:18:56.180 --> 00:18:59.210
And it's really important to
have that two-way communication

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:01.490
between frontline
workers and HQ,

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:04.250
because again, they have
that wealth of information

00:19:04.250 --> 00:19:06.390
and knowledge about
what's going on.

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:09.080
And then with things like Gmail,
Hangouts, and the new product

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:11.210
currents, you can also
make sure that you're

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:13.340
having that two-way
communication

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:15.410
and keeping employees engaged.

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:17.150
With the Google
Admin console, you

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:22.340
can manage both Chromebooks and
G Suite from the same place.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:26.210
And G Suite is built with
powerful Google search and AI

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:28.090
so it's faster to
get things done.

00:19:31.790 --> 00:19:34.010
The solution that brings
this all together,

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:37.370
one of the new solutions that
we introduced a few months ago

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:40.930
with our partners, is Grab
and Go with Chrome Enterprise.

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:44.330
Now, we develop this here at
Google as a loaner program.

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:48.230
So if my device breaks
down, I go to a shelf

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:49.940
stacked with Chromebooks.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:51.260
I pick up a Chromebook.

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:52.100
I sign in.

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:53.480
I use it for the day.

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:55.830
No interaction with IT needed.

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:58.700
And then when I'm
done with it, I simply

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:02.180
mark that I'm returning it, sign
out, put it back on the shelf,

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:04.310
and then it's ready for
the next person to pick up.

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:07.220
Again, no IT involvement needed.

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:10.250
This means that I have
24 access to that,

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:15.060
and that it saves IT a
bunch of time and expense.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:16.890
So this is used for
a loaner program,

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:19.990
but you can imagine using
it for a shift worker.

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:21.830
A call center employee comes in.

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:23.480
They pick up the
device they need.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:25.580
When they're done,
the next shift starts,

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:27.650
and they could pick
up those same devices.

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:30.220
That helps you have
less devices on hand.

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:35.060
It also saves IT money and time
in getting those shift workers

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:38.290
the devices that they need.

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:40.840
So I've talked a lot
about Chrome Enterprise

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:43.700
and the benefits that it
has for frontline workers.

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:47.590
Now I want to go through some
actual use cases and customers

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:50.500
that we have seen
implement Chrome devices

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:51.625
to their frontline workers.

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:57.160
So let's start with the first
one of digitizing, engagement,

00:20:57.160 --> 00:20:58.510
and training.

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:01.660
I think we all know how
important training is,

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:05.260
but it can be difficult when you
have a decentralized employee

00:21:05.260 --> 00:21:05.840
base.

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:07.990
So think about people
who are working

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:10.220
in a retail environment,
for instance.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:11.650
They don't have
access to come in

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:14.030
to HQ when they need training.

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:17.310
So you need to be able to deploy
training to these employees.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:20.170
And a lot of employees don't
have one-to-one devices to just

00:21:20.170 --> 00:21:23.060
be able to do that
training on their own time.

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:25.310
So you can either have
a pool of devices--

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:28.000
and we see a lot of customers
actually start with Chromebooks

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:31.150
here because it's an isolated
environment that they

00:21:31.150 --> 00:21:35.660
can put these devices in without
a lot of change management.

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:39.310
We also see retail and
food service companies

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:42.430
have these devices in the
back office which then allows

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:44.260
employees to go to
the back of the office

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:47.140
when they have a chance,
get their training,

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:50.590
fill out their HR paperwork, do
other things along those lines

00:21:50.590 --> 00:21:52.840
on those back office devices.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:56.440
And one of those customers
is Panda Express.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:22:00.130
Panda Express recently adopted
Chromebooks in its back office

00:22:00.130 --> 00:22:02.230
so that its employees
could get training

00:22:02.230 --> 00:22:05.530
to make your favorites, like
orange chicken and Kung Pao

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:08.410
chicken, so that they have
a consistent experience

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:11.380
across every single
one of their locations.

00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:13.970
And these employees can
use it for training.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:15.490
They can also use
the same device

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:18.580
to log in to their
Office 365 environment

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:22.180
if they need to do more
productivity-oriented tasks.

00:22:22.180 --> 00:22:24.880
And they work with their
partner, their channel partner,

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:28.510
to create an application
that allows employees

00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:31.750
to use these training
modules offline because some

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:38.680
of the locations have Wi-Fi that
isn't as consistent as others.

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:41.280
Another area that we've
seen a lot of adoption

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:44.250
is improving
back-of-house processes.

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:48.150
So think about manufacturers
on the line who

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:50.490
need the latest
product specs or people

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:52.290
who are in a warehouse
who are shipping

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:54.210
and need that
information about what

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:58.860
they need to be shipping really
instantly and seamlessly.

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:00.930
A lot of this is
actually today being

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:04.170
done on paper-based processes.

00:23:04.170 --> 00:23:07.740
And that's just simply
insecure, and it also

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:10.980
causes things to be out of date.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:14.160
There's only so many times you
can update a paper before you

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:15.600
give it to a shift worker.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.210
And things could even
change during a shift.

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:21.140
So one of our
customers is Sanmina.

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:24.360
They're a global manufacturing
and supply chain company.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:27.570
They manufacture a variety
of products, including

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.993
intensive care unit monitors.

00:23:29.993 --> 00:23:31.410
And it's really
important for them

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:35.640
to have version control for
these types of ICU monitors

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:37.050
because they're critical.

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:38.280
Right?

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:41.940
So they moved all 75
of their factories

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:46.630
from a paper base manufacturing
to Chrome devices.

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:48.450
And now I'm going
to show you a video

00:23:48.450 --> 00:23:50.250
of how Sanmina has done this.

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:57.580
CARL DUCKETT: Sanmina
is a world-class global

00:23:57.580 --> 00:23:59.735
manufacturing and
supply chain company.

00:23:59.735 --> 00:24:02.110
You may never have heard of
us, but the products we build

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:03.850
are extremely high reliability.

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:06.250
Whether it's a warfighter
or it's someone

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:09.712
in ICU in the hospital,
people's lives depend on them.

00:24:09.712 --> 00:24:11.170
If something happens,
we need to be

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:13.530
able to understand how
that product was built.

00:24:13.530 --> 00:24:16.150
20 years ago, you had a lot
of information, but none of it

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:16.930
was useful.

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:19.840
It was sorting through file
cabinets and folders and paper.

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:21.580
One of the biggest
transformations for us

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:23.770
on our manufacturing
floor was going

00:24:23.770 --> 00:24:26.740
from paper-based manufacturing
process instructions

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:29.210
to a cloud-based system.

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:31.690
The importance of
document control

00:24:31.690 --> 00:24:34.780
is huge when you build
high reliability product.

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:37.300
Today we have
everything stored safely

00:24:37.300 --> 00:24:39.550
and smartly in the cloud.

00:24:39.550 --> 00:24:42.280
So Chrome is a cloud-first
operating system

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:44.750
that powers our operations.

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:46.730
A Chrome-based
system is much better

00:24:46.730 --> 00:24:48.140
than where we were previously.

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:51.380
The hardware is cheaper, and
also, the cost of data security

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:53.610
is a piece that you
can't even quantify.

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:56.180
Within Sanmina, we've
got 75 factories.

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:58.340
It's hard to walk around
a factory in Thailand

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:02.120
and a factory in
Ireland in the same day.

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:05.180
With Chrome OS and 42Q
being closely integrated

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:07.760
throughout our factories,
we're able to do real time data

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:09.630
analytics anywhere in the world.

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:12.220
They really are the backbone
of everything we do at Sanmina.

00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:15.230
And Chrome OS is the operating
system that it runs on.

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:19.180
So Chrome is empowering
everyone to be a cloud worker.

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:20.620
With the help of
Chrome OS, we're

00:25:20.620 --> 00:25:22.690
a lot more efficient
and a lot more secure

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:23.793
than we were before.

00:25:23.793 --> 00:25:25.210
When you see the
products we build

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:26.812
that really do change
people's lives,

00:25:26.812 --> 00:25:28.270
it makes it emotional
and personal.

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:38.110
LAURA JONES: So let's talk
about customer contact centers.

00:25:38.110 --> 00:25:43.970
These are highly important to
having great customer service.

00:25:43.970 --> 00:25:47.020
If you think about when
you most recently called up

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:48.700
a contact center,
you were probably

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:50.200
frustrated about some things.

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:52.300
That's what led
you to call them.

00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:54.190
But someone who's on
the other line that's

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:56.020
really knowledgeable
that can get

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:58.480
you your information
straight away,

00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:00.520
that could probably
change the way that you

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:03.010
think about that
incident and help

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:05.360
save the day for that brand.

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:07.120
So it's important
that contact centers

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:12.160
have technology that's really
easy to use, that is simple

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:14.060
and that's fast.

00:26:14.060 --> 00:26:15.710
And we've seen a
lot of companies

00:26:15.710 --> 00:26:18.200
adopt Chrome Enterprise
for their contact centers

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:20.300
for these reasons.

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:22.890
One of our customers,
Mercado Libre,

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:26.030
they're a company that
operates online marketplaces.

00:26:26.030 --> 00:26:28.190
They were struggling
with the Windows machines

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:31.260
that they were using because
of the long boot times.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:33.920
In fact, sometimes they
would take up to five minutes

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:35.470
to boot a device.

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:37.910
And in an hourly
shift environment,

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:40.850
five minutes is just
five minutes too long.

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:45.770
So Mercado Libre, with the
help of our partner Neverware,

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:50.450
updated their existing
hardware with Chrome OS.

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:52.670
And now, with the
six-second boot time,

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:58.500
they're able to save
215 minutes per shift

00:26:58.500 --> 00:27:00.900
across all of their employees.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:02.490
It's a lot of time saved.

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:05.490
It also helps the employees
start their day off better,

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:07.740
perhaps be happier.

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:10.740
In addition to that, because
of the cloud-native nature

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:13.680
of the operating
system, Mercado Libre

00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:16.750
can have their associates
work from anywhere.

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.650
So in case of a
disaster, or in case

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:23.070
they lose power at their
contact center headquarters,

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:25.980
these associates are able
to work from home using

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:28.920
Chrome operating system because
they can just sign in and get

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:31.740
their entire profile there
for them on whatever device

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:36.480
that they have, whatever Chrome
device that they have on hand.

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:38.160
The next area that
I want to talk about

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:40.300
is front-of-house workers.

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:43.840
So these are the workers that
are in front of your customers.

00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:46.900
They're the ones
helping out picking

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:49.710
a dress for a special
occasion or helping

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:52.800
them sign up for a bank
account in this instance.

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:56.520
And front-of-house workers,
they need technology

00:27:56.520 --> 00:28:00.180
at their fingertips right away
to be able to help customers.

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:01.710
We did a survey
with Forrester that

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:05.250
found one of the top pain
points for frontline workers

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:07.200
with technology
is that they have

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:10.410
to leave their customers in
the middle of an interaction

00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:11.850
to go find information.

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:15.090
It just makes for a bad
experience for everyone.

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:17.160
So you can put
technology into the hands

00:28:17.160 --> 00:28:20.040
of your front-of-house
workers, give them

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:24.090
laptops or tablets on the floor,
but you can also help them out

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:27.360
by offloading some of
that work onto customers

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:29.130
with customer self-service.

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:31.260
And in fact, a lot
of customers like

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:35.790
serving themselves before they
need to talk to an associate.

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:37.590
Charles Schwab did this.

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:40.380
When they were introducing
their new Schwab Intelligent

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:43.140
Portfolios, they wanted
to introduce a customer

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:45.930
self-service solution
into their branches that

00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:49.710
was easy to use for someone
of any technical ability, that

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:50.880
was easy to manage.

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:53.470
Because they have lots
of different branches,

00:28:53.470 --> 00:28:55.890
and they didn't want
their associates

00:28:55.890 --> 00:28:57.600
to have to troubleshoot.

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:01.620
And so they introduced Chrome
devices as this solution.

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:03.600
Now when someone
walks into a branch

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:06.090
and wants to learn more
about these portfolios,

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:08.310
an associate can hand
them a Chromebook.

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:10.470
They can explore the
offerings, and then they

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:12.840
can do the application
straight from there.

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:16.240
This helps cut down this
time for the associate,

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:19.200
and it also has helped to
eliminate some common errors

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:22.590
that might happen if you're
doing two-way communication

00:29:22.590 --> 00:29:27.085
to fill out an application.

00:29:27.085 --> 00:29:29.210
The last one that I want
to talk to you about today

00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:31.190
is information on the go.

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:34.520
Now, think about field
workers or mobile workers

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:38.990
who need to be able to tap into
a device wherever they are.

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:40.790
They need a reliable device.

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:44.360
They need a device that can
give them the information they

00:29:44.360 --> 00:29:46.130
need very quickly.

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:51.170
And nowhere else is this
more apparent than for EMTs.

00:29:51.170 --> 00:29:53.545
And Middlesex Hospital,
one of our customers,

00:29:53.545 --> 00:29:55.670
they were having trouble
with the devices that they

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:57.620
were using for their EMTs.

00:29:57.620 --> 00:29:59.390
They were breaking down a lot.

00:29:59.390 --> 00:30:01.670
They weren't available
in those crucial seconds

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:04.170
that EMTs need to
get information.

00:30:04.170 --> 00:30:06.360
And so they adopted
Chrome devices.

00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:08.360
And now I'm going to show
you a video about how

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:12.420
Middlesex has helped their EMT
workers with Chrome devices.

