00:33:06.230 --> 00:33:09.300
And we're back with
frequently asked questions.

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:12.380
So the first one-- can we
invest in Chrome hardware

00:33:12.380 --> 00:33:16.160
while still maintaining
our Office 365 environment?

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:18.020
The answer is yes, you can.

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:20.690
And I gave you the example
about Panda Express that's

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:25.130
using Office 365 with
their Chrome devices

00:33:25.130 --> 00:33:27.230
for their frontline workers.

00:33:27.230 --> 00:33:29.780
The thing that I would
recommend is figure out

00:33:29.780 --> 00:33:33.620
how your employees
are using Office 365.

00:33:33.620 --> 00:33:35.810
They can access the
Android application,

00:33:35.810 --> 00:33:38.210
and they can access
the web version.

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:41.630
If that's enough-- and it
should be enough for almost all

00:33:41.630 --> 00:33:43.910
frontline workers, especially--

00:33:43.910 --> 00:33:46.760
then it should be
as easy as just

00:33:46.760 --> 00:33:50.150
letting them access via the
Android app or the web app.

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:53.010
No special updates needed there.

00:33:53.010 --> 00:33:57.590
So yes, you can use Office
365 on a Chromebook.

00:33:57.590 --> 00:33:59.330
The second question--
is there any way

00:33:59.330 --> 00:34:02.450
to restrict access so that
employees only have access

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:04.490
to certain applications?

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:05.820
Yes, you can.

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:07.830
So there are a few things
to talk about here.

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:10.790
One is manage Google Play Store.

00:34:10.790 --> 00:34:14.389
So you can whitelist and
blacklist applications

00:34:14.389 --> 00:34:16.159
so that employees
only have access

00:34:16.159 --> 00:34:18.530
to certain applications
in Google Play,

00:34:18.530 --> 00:34:21.860
and then you can also blacklist
so that they can't download.

00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:24.170
But often, what our
customers are doing

00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:26.100
is going even further than that.

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:28.940
And you can very
easily administer

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:33.230
policies that lock a device
down to just a few applications.

00:34:33.230 --> 00:34:34.639
And we see this happening a lot.

00:34:34.639 --> 00:34:38.659
So if you, for instance,
are in a warehouse--

00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:41.370
we have a customer,
Stitch Fix, who does this.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:45.199
They have certain applications
that employees on the warehouse

00:34:45.199 --> 00:34:47.989
use for their day-to-day
work, and then

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:50.900
they also have applications
for things like time sheets

00:34:50.900 --> 00:34:56.469
or any of the HR administration
that they need to do as well.

00:34:56.469 --> 00:34:58.590
What are our options
to have users sign in

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:01.710
without an identity
for temporary access?

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:03.420
Well, I'm glad you asked.

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:05.160
We have Manage Guest Session.

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:07.320
And this allows
people to sign in

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:12.150
without using identity while
still giving you full access

00:35:12.150 --> 00:35:17.140
to manage the device, the
applications, and the browser.

00:35:17.140 --> 00:35:21.030
So we have our customers who
are doing a lot of self-service

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:25.050
with their own customers
using Manage Guest Session.

00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:27.420
And you can also use it in
those sorts of environments

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:31.800
where maybe you have one
device that's there for people

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:33.930
to do more administration
tasks where they

00:35:33.930 --> 00:35:35.710
don't need to use an identity.

00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:38.550
You can just set the device
up with Manage Guest Session

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:41.880
through the Google
Admin Console.

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:45.000
How do customers currently
deal with offline capabilities

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:48.080
or limited Wi-Fi situations?

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:50.910
So we'll first start with one
thing, which is to myth bust

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:54.450
the fact that Chromebooks
don't work offline.

00:35:54.450 --> 00:35:55.800
Chromebooks do work offline.

00:35:55.800 --> 00:35:58.830
I use mine often
while I'm traveling

00:35:58.830 --> 00:36:03.240
on airplanes when I don't
have access to great Wi-Fi.

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:06.090
You can use GSuite,
Gmail offline,

00:36:06.090 --> 00:36:09.140
and there's also certain
applications that work offline.

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:11.520
Some of our customers
do a couple of things.

00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:15.480
One, there are Chrome devices
that are enabled with LTE,

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:17.380
so that is an option.

00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:18.960
You could use a Wi-Fi hotspot.

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:21.060
So that's something
that Middlesex Hospital

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:22.830
does for their EMTs.

00:36:22.830 --> 00:36:24.480
And then the
customer example that

00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:26.900
I gave with Panda Express--

00:36:26.900 --> 00:36:28.770
they're using one
of their partners

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:31.140
to create an application
for their training

00:36:31.140 --> 00:36:32.120
that works offline.

00:36:32.120 --> 00:36:36.553
And this way, they don't
have the dependency on Wi-Fi.

00:36:36.553 --> 00:36:37.720
And then the last question--

00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:40.880
MacOS and Windows already
provide regular updates.

00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:43.280
So what's the difference
with Chrome OS?

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:45.850
So Chrome OS not only
provides regular updates,

00:36:45.850 --> 00:36:47.740
but one it does it
in the background.

00:36:47.740 --> 00:36:51.010
So this way, you don't have
to worry about your employees

00:36:51.010 --> 00:36:53.590
accepting those updates.

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:56.560
They just happen, so you
don't have that delay.

00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:58.210
You don't have to
worry about the fact

00:36:58.210 --> 00:37:01.600
that someone has just been
delinquent on updating

00:37:01.600 --> 00:37:02.820
their device.

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:05.680
And they also happen every
six weeks consistently.

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:08.800
You can choose whether or not
you want to implement those

00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:09.650
every six weeks.

00:37:09.650 --> 00:37:12.040
But if you do, that
helps to mitigate

00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:15.650
any issues a lot quicker.

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:17.640
So that's all the
questions that we have.

